At sea level in Mumbai or Miami, every breath pulls in plenty of oxygen. Air still has the same mix of gases high up, but the air is thinner, so each breath holds less. At 5,100 m, each lungful carries only about half the oxygen it would at the seaside.

Visitors often get headaches, feel sick and can't sleep. This is altitude sickness. Climbing slowly gives the body time to adjust, by making more red [[@blood|blood]] cells to carry oxygen. Some visitors to La Paz's airport, at over 4,000 m, feel dizzy the moment they step off the plane.

{{story:altitude}}

Every living thing takes in carbon, from the air or from food. A tiny share of it is [[radioactive|radioactive]] carbon-14. While a plant or animal is alive, it keeps topping up. When it dies, the topping up stops, and the carbon-14 slowly fades away. After 5,730 years, half is gone.

The American chemist Willard Libby worked this out in the late 1940s and won a Nobel Prize for it. Carbon dating changed [[@archaeology|archaeology]] overnight. It has been used on Ötzi the Iceman, found in the Alps, who lived about 5,300 years ago. Charcoal and grain from the [[@indus-cities|Indus cities]] have been dated the same way, helping [[archaeologist|archaeologists]] build a timeline of when they grew and declined.

{{story:marie-curie}}

Crushed rock alone is not soil. The next ingredient is dead and rotting material: fallen leaves, roots, dead insects and animal droppings. Fungi and bacteria break it down into a dark, spongy substance called [[humus|humus]]. Humus holds water like a sponge and is rich in the food plants need.

A single teaspoon of healthy soil holds more living things than there are people on Earth, mostly bacteria too small to see. Fungi spread through soil in long white threads, linking plant roots. The [[@mushrooms|mushrooms]] you see above ground are only the fruit of these hidden networks.

{{story:earthworms}}

पोर्ट ब्लेअरच्या ईशान्येला सुमारे 135 किमीवर, [[@andaman|अंदमान]] समुद्रात असलेलं बॅरन बेट हा भारतातला एकमेव जागृत ज्वालामुखी आहे. शंभराहून जास्त वर्षं शांत राहिल्यानंतर 1991 मध्ये त्याचा पुन्हा उद्रेक झाला, आणि त्यानंतरही अनेकदा तो जागा झाला आहे. तिथे कोणी माणूस राहत नाही, पण रानटी शेळ्यांचा एक कळप मात्र राहतो. शास्त्रज्ञ बोटीने जाऊन समुद्रातूनच त्याचा अभ्यास करतात. आणखी उत्तरेला नारकोंडम बेट आहे. तोही एक ज्वालामुखी, पण फार फार काळापासून शांत.

भारताची सर्वांत मोठी ज्वालामुखीची गोष्ट मात्र याहून कितीतरी जुनी आहे. सुमारे 6 कोटी 60 लाख वर्षांपूर्वी पश्चिम भारतातल्या प्रचंड भेगांमधून पुन्हा पुन्हा लाव्हा बाहेर पडला. हे प्रवाह एकमेकांवर रचले जाऊन [[@deccan|दख्खनचे लाव्हा थर]] (डेक्कन ट्रॅप) तयार झाले. काही ठिकाणी हे थर 2 किमीहून जास्त जाड आहेत. सह्याद्रीच्या कड्यांवर दिसणारे काळ्या [[basalt|बेसॉल्ट]] दगडाचे थरावर थर याच लाव्ह्याचे. कापसासाठी उत्तम असलेली महाराष्ट्राची [[@soil-types-india|काळी कसदार माती]] याच लाव्ह्याच्या खडकाची झीज होऊन तयार झाली.

{{story:pompeii}}

In 1984, an American scientist named Katy Payne was watching Asian elephants at a zoo in Portland, Oregon. Standing beside their enclosure, she kept feeling a strange throbbing in the air, like the deep rumble of a pipe organ she remembered from her childhood church. She could feel it, but not quite hear it.

Payne came back with recording equipment that could pick up very low sounds. When the tapes were sped up, they were full of elephant calls nobody had known about. Elephants were talking in [[@sound-travel|sounds]] below 20 hertz, too deep for human ears. Later, she and her colleagues showed that wild African elephants use these calls to find each other over several kilometres.

{{story:whale-songs}}

In Vijayapura, once called Bijapur, in [[@karnataka|Karnataka]], stands [[@india-wonder-gol-gumbaz|the Gol Gumbaz]], the tomb of Sultan Muhammad Adil Shah, completed in the mid-1600s. Its dome is about 44 metres across on the inside, one of the largest in the world from that time.

Running round the base of the dome is a narrow balcony called the whispering gallery. A whisper on one side can be heard clearly on the other, as the sound creeps round the curved wall. A clap or a shout bounces around the dome and comes back several times. Visitors' voices fill the space with echoes, so it is rarely quiet. St Paul's Cathedral in London has a famous whispering gallery that works the same way.

{{story:dolphins-click}}

In 1947, the Hungarian-born engineer Dennis Gabor, working in England, came up with the idea while trying to improve [[electron|electron]] [[microscope|microscopes]]. He named it hologram, from Greek words meaning "whole message". But he couldn't make good ones. Ordinary light is a jumble of different waves, and holograms need light where all the waves march in step.

That kind of light arrived with the [[@lasers|laser]] in 1960. Within a few years, scientists in the USA and the Soviet Union made the first sharp 3D holograms. Gabor won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1971.

{{story:lasers}}

Throwing sticks are among the oldest flying tools ever made. The oldest known boomerang was carved from a mammoth's tusk and found in Obłazowa Cave in southern Poland. In 2025, scientists dated it to about 40,000 years old. Like many ancient [[@stone-tools|stone and bone tools]], it was made long before anyone farmed or built a town.

Ancient Egyptians also used throwing sticks. Several were buried with the boy king Tutankhamun, including a few that may have been made to return.

{{story:space-station}}

The Channel Tunnel joins England and France. It is about 50 kilometres long, and almost 38 kilometres of it lie under the sea. Teams dug from both sides, through a layer of chalky rock that was soft enough to cut but tight enough to keep water out. In 1990, the two sides broke through to each other less than a metre apart. Trains began running in 1994.

[[@kolkata|Kolkata]] now has India's first underwater metro. Its twin tunnels pass beneath the Hooghly river, joining Howrah to the city centre. Trains began carrying passengers in 2024, and they cross under the river in under a minute.

{{story:earthworms}}

The white space between panels is called the gutter. Nothing is drawn there, yet that is where a lot of the story happens. Your mind fills in the action that links one picture to the next.

The American cartoonist Scott McCloud explained this in his book *Understanding Comics*, itself written as a comic. He called it closure. It's the same thing your brain does when it watches drawings flicker past in an [[@animation-how|animated cartoon]], joining separate moments into movement.

{{story:animation-how}}

In Rajasthan, kathputli puppeteers have performed for centuries. *Kath* means wood and *putli* means doll. The puppets have [[@wood-carving|carved wooden heads]], big painted eyes and bright clothes, with long skirts instead of legs.

The puppeteer holds strings tied to the head and hands, and makes the figures dance, fight on horseback and bow to kings. Often a musician plays a drum and sings while the puppeteer speaks for the characters, sometimes using a small reed whistle held in the mouth to make a squeaky puppet voice. Many shows tell tales of [[@rajputs|Rajput heroes]] like Amar Singh Rathore.

{{story:ramayana}}

In December 1965, Mary Beth Tinker, aged 13, and a few other students in Des Moines, Iowa, wore black armbands to school to mourn those killed in the Vietnam War. They were suspended. Her family went to court.

In 1969 the US Supreme Court ruled in their favour. Students, it said, do not "shed their constitutional rights to freedom of speech or expression at the schoolhouse gate". Schools can still stop speech that seriously disrupts lessons, but they can't silence a quiet protest just because they dislike it.

{{story:speeches-famous}}

A modern phone shows how spread out making things has become. Its design might come from California or South Korea. Its chips may be made in Taiwan, its screen in Korea or China, its rare metals mined in Africa or South America. It is then put together in a factory in China, Vietnam or, more and more, India.

Many of the [[@smartphones|smartphones]] sold worldwide are now assembled in India, some for [[export|export]]. Parts and finished goods travel in steel containers on giant ships, through busy [[@sea-ports|sea ports]] such as Mumbai's Nhava Sheva or Los Angeles.

{{story:sea-ports}}

[[@australia|Australia]]'s Don Bradman played 52 Test matches between 1928 and 1948. He scored 6,996 runs and was dismissed 70 times, giving an average of 99.94. Most of the greatest batters in history average somewhere in the 50s in Tests, so Bradman stands far apart.

The story of his last innings is famous. Before his final Test, at The Oval in London in 1948, he had been dismissed 69 times. If he scored just 4 runs, even if he then got out, his average would be 7,000 ÷ 70 = exactly 100. He was bowled second ball for a duck by the English leg-spinner Eric Hollies, and his average stopped at 99.94.

{{story:test-cricket}}

On 18 June 1980, at Imperial College London, a computer picked two random 13-digit numbers: 7,686,369,774,870 and 2,465,099,745,779. Shakuntala Devi was asked to multiply them.

She gave the full 26-digit answer, 18,947,668,177,995,426,462,773,730, in 28 seconds. The time included saying all those digits out loud. The feat was listed in the 1982 Guinness Book of Records. To get a sense of the size, that answer is far bigger than the number of grains of sand on a beach, a scale explored in [[@big-numbers|big numbers]].

{{story:memory}}

[[@brahmagupta|Brahmagupta]] was an [[astronomer|astronomer]] in Rajasthan. Alongside his rules for adding and multiplying with zero, he treated [[@negative-numbers|negative numbers]] as "debts" and positive numbers as "fortunes". A debt minus zero is a debt, he wrote.

Even he wasn't sure about dividing by zero. He said zero divided by zero was zero, which mathematicians no longer accept. Today we say dividing by zero has no answer at all.

{{story:aztecs-maya}}

The organisers changed the look of the game. Muddy courts became blue and orange mats. Players got numbered shirts and bright team kits. Matches were shortened to 40 minutes, with two halves of 20, so they fit neatly into an evening TV slot.

They also tweaked the rules to keep games tense. If a team fails to score on two raids in a row, the next is a "do-or-die" raid: score or lose a point. When a defence is down to three or fewer players and still stops the raider, it is a super tackle worth two points. Knock out the whole opposing team and you earn an "all out" bonus.

{{story:muscles}}

The classic black-and-white football has 32 panels: 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons. This pattern, used on the Adidas Telstar at the 1970 World Cup in Mexico, makes a shape mathematicians call a truncated icosahedron. It's very close to a sphere, which is why it rolls so well. [[@shapes-3d|Shapes]] like this show up in chemistry too.

For decades, many hand-stitched footballs came from Sialkot, in Pakistan, and from Jalandhar in [[@punjab|Punjab]], India, which still make large numbers of balls today.

{{story:shapes-3d}}

Enamel is the hardest material in your body, harder than bone. But it is made of minerals, and acid can dissolve minerals. Once the acid in your mouth gets strong enough, minerals start leaving the enamel.

At first, a chalky white spot appears. At this stage the damage can still reverse, if acid attacks become less frequent and fluoride helps minerals return. If the attacks continue, the spot turns brown and finally breaks into a hole. Under the enamel lies softer dentine, which decays faster. When decay reaches the nerve in the centre of the tooth, it can cause a sharp toothache.

{{story:toothbrush}}

In 1936, aged 24, Turing wrote a paper about which maths problems can be solved by following fixed rules. To answer it, he imagined a machine reading and writing symbols on an endless strip of tape, following a short list of instructions.

He showed that one such machine, given the right instructions, could copy what any other did. That is the idea behind a computer that can run any program. Every [[@algorithms|algorithm]] on your phone fits inside it.

{{story:algorithms}}

Surgery is old. In ancient India, [[@sushruta|Sushruta]] described dozens of surgical instruments and operations, including repairing noses. Over the centuries, [[@doctors-history|doctors]] added [[anaesthesia|anaesthesia]], which keeps patients asleep, and germ-killing methods that made operations far safer.

{{story:sushruta}}

Robots are the latest step. They are expensive, though. A single system can cost crores of rupees, plus costly instruments for every operation. Doctors still argue about which operations really benefit, because for some, robotic and ordinary keyhole surgery give similar results.

Before dawn, police arrested opposition leaders across the country, including JP, Morarji Desai, Atal Bihari Vajpayee and L.K. Advani. Many were held under a law called MISA, which allowed detention without trial.

Electricity to some newspaper offices in Delhi was cut that night, so they could not print. Censorship rules followed. Some editors protested in quiet ways. One newspaper printed a blank space where its editorial should have been. Foreign [[@journalism|reporters]] who would not accept the rules were sent out of the country.

{{story:free-speech}}

Babur died in 1530. His son Humayun lost the empire to the Afghan ruler Sher Shah Suri and spent fifteen years in exile. Sher Shah used his short reign well, building roads, rest houses and a new silver coin called the rupiya, ancestor of today's rupee.

Humayun won back Delhi in 1555 but died months later, falling down the stairs of his library. His tomb in Delhi, with its garden laid out in four parts, inspired the Taj Mahal.

{{story:taj-mahal}}

Dorothy Vaughan joined Langley in 1943. In 1949, she became the head of the West Area Computers, one of the first Black supervisors there.

When electronic computers arrived in the late 1950s, she saw they would replace human calculation. Instead of waiting to lose her job, she taught herself and her team a programming language called FORTRAN. Many of her computers became some of NASA's early programmers. She is a good example of someone who watched the future coming and walked towards it.

{{story:first-computers}}

In 1964, Prime Minister [[@lal-bahadur-shastri|Lal Bahadur Shastri]] visited Anand and was impressed. He asked Kurien to repeat the model nationwide. The National Dairy Development Board was set up in 1965 with Kurien as chairman.

Operation Flood began in 1970. Dairy products donated by Europe were sold in India, and the money paid for village cooperatives, chilling centres and dairies. Milk tankers and trains carried milk from rural areas to cities. Over about 26 years, the programme reached tens of thousands of villages.

{{story:cows-india}}

Things float when they push aside, or displace, a weight of water equal to their own weight. This is called [[buoyancy|buoyancy]]. A steel ship floats this way, by pushing aside a lot of water with its wide hull.

{{story:floating-ships}}

In fresh water, your body has to sink almost completely before it has pushed aside enough water. In Dead Sea water, a litre weighs about 1.24 kg instead of 1 kg. You only need to sink part of the way before the water holds you up, so you float high. Visitors lie back, legs in the air, and some cover themselves in black mud from the shore. The minerals and mud are also sold around the world for skincare.

In about 1199, Qutb-ud-din Aibak began a tall tower of red sandstone. His successor Iltutmish added more storeys. Today the Qutb Minar is about 73 m tall, roughly as high as a 24-storey building, and covered in carved writing.

Next to it stands the [[@mystery-iron-pillar|Iron Pillar]], about 7 m tall, made in the time of the Gupta kings. It has barely [[@rust|rusted]] in about 1,600 years, which puzzled scientists for a long time. The answer turned out to be the special make-up of its iron.

{{story:rust}}

In the old city of Fez, a woman named Fatima al-Fihri used her inheritance to found a mosque called al-Qarawiyyin in 859. It grew into a centre of learning, teaching religion, law, astronomy and mathematics. UNESCO and Guinness World Records describe it as the [[@hist-first-university|oldest university in the world]] still operating. Its library, among the [[@great-libraries|great libraries]] of the Islamic world, still holds very old handwritten books.

Fez itself has one of the largest car-free old towns anywhere. Goods are carried through its thousands of lanes by donkeys and handcarts. In the Chouara tannery, workers dye leather in honeycomb rows of stone pits full of colour.

{{story:ibn-battuta}}

The castle has towers, turrets and a throne room that looks like a Byzantine church, with a golden apse and a huge chandelier. The singers' hall is decorated with scenes from legends.

Behind the walls, though, it was very up to date for the 1880s. It had warm-air central heating, running water on every floor, flushing toilets, an electric bell system for calling servants, and a telephone. The kitchen had a roasting spit turned by the heat rising from the fire.

{{story:fairy-tales}}

Each new wave of people added dishes. German immigrants brought sausages that became hot dogs. Italian families in New York and Chicago turned simple Neapolitan pizza into something huge and cheesy. Jewish bakers made bagels a [[@breakfast-world|New York breakfast]]. Chinese workers, many of whom came for the [[@gold-rush|gold rush]] and the railways, [[@restaurants-history|opened restaurants]] that served chop suey.

Enslaved Africans and their [[descendant|descendants]] shaped Southern cooking deeply. They brought knowledge of rice farming and foods such as okra and black-eyed peas, and developed what is now called soul food. Today you can find dosa in New Jersey, pho in Texas and [[@tacos|tacos]] almost everywhere.

{{story:ellis-island}}

Most water bottles are made of a [[@plastic-made|plastic]] called PET. In 2024, scientists at Columbia University used a new laser method to look for very tiny plastic particles. They found on average about 240,000 bits in each litre of bottled water, most of them far smaller than a speck of dust.

{{story:ocean-plastic}}

Nobody yet knows whether these particles harm people. Scientists are studying the question. What is clear is that bottles themselves add up. Many end up in [[@pollution-plastic-land|landfills]], rivers and the [[@ocean-plastic|ocean]] instead of being recycled.
